[StudyName "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️"]
[ChapterName "Damiano Defense"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/e01yoGqe/LztgG0lb"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Water-Flame"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.18"]
[UTCTime "18:42:38"]

{ The Damiano Defense (e4 e5 Nf3 f6) is one of the worst openings for black. While it may look like the move f6 guards black's pawn on e5, it actually does not, and it opens up black's kingside, which white can easily exploit. Despite being a terrible opening, the Damiano Defense is played commonly by beginners. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 { White attacks black's pawn on e5. Black has a few good ways to defend his pawn, such as Nc6 or d6, but instead, black will play f6. } { [%cal Rf3e5] } 2... f6 { Now this may look OK for black. It doesn't look great since the knight is blocked from its best square and it opens up the kingside. But it doesn't look like anything terrible is gonna happen soon. However, that is far from the truth. } { [%cal Bf6e5] } 3. Nxe5 { White's move here may look terrible. It gives up the knight, just for a pawn? What is white thinking?! Well, if black recaptures, he will be in for a world of pain. } 3... fxe5 { Now, white can take advantage of the open kingside. Can you spot the move? } (3... Qe7 { This is black's only hope. This move will regain the pawn, but white will be left with a far superior position. However, since this opening is played almost exclusively by beginners, it's not likely that they'll know of this trap. }) 4. Qh5+ { And now black is in a REALLY bad position. If he blocks the check with g6, the queen can then take the pawn on e5, giving check and forking the rook. So, black's only other option would be to move the king to e7. } { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rh5e5,Rh5e8] } 4... Ke7 (4... g6 5. Qxe5+ Qe7 6. Qxh8 { And the rook is lost. }) 5. Qxe5+ { [%cal Re5e7] } 5... Kf7 6. Bc4+ { And black is in deep water now. There are a couple of ways black can save himself from checkmate, but none are desirable. } { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rc4f7] } 6... d5 { [%csl Gc8][%cal Gc8f5] } (6... Kg6 { The move d5 may look like it's giving a pawn away, but it's actually black's only way to prevent checkmate. } 7. Qf5+ Kh6 8. h4 Qf6 { Black can try to trade off queens, but it won't work. White can deliver checkmate in a few moves here. } 9. d4+ g5 10. Bxg5+ Kg7 11. Bxf6+ Nxf6 12. Qg5# { And the game is over. So this is why black should play d5 before moving the king to g6. }) 7. Bxd5+ { And now black has to move his king to g6. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rd5f7] } 7... Kg6 8. h4 h6 { This move gives black an escape for his king after white plays h5. } 9. Bxb7 { This move may look like it's giving up the bishop, but if black takes, white can give checkmate in 1 move. At this point, black is pretty much lost. } { [%cal Rb7c8,Rb7a8] } 9... Bd6 { [%cal Rd6e5] } 10. Qa5 Nf6 11. Bxa8 { And, black has lost his rook, as well as an exposed king and terrible overall position. White clearly has a won position. } *


