{ This is pretty rare, as most white opponents won't make these moves, but some beginners will occasionally do this. It's just good to know in case someone actually does do this. }
1. g4 { This move is occasionally played by many players; even a few GMs have used it. It's an extremely risky move that all but destroys white's kingside defense. However, it can give white some good attacking chances. } 1... e5 { A lot of players do d5 instead of this, attacking the pawn on g4. I'd recommend this move over d5 even if you're not aiming for the 2-move checkmate. } 2. f3 { Now notice how there's a clear diagonal to white's king. The next move is pretty easy to spot. } { [%cal Yh4e1] } 2... Qh4# { And just like that, game over in 2 moves. This position can also be reached by white moving f3 first. This will also work if white does f4 instead of f3. } { [%cal Rh4e1] } *


