[StudyName "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️"]
[ChapterName "Parham's Attack"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/e01yoGqe/PCSJClQN"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Water-Flame"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.18"]
[UTCTime "22:40:42"]

{ This opening is one of the most common among beginners. White hopes for black to fall into a trap within the first few moves. One trap leads to the loss of a rook; the other to losing the game in 4 moves. }
1. e4 { White opens with e4, which is arguably the best first move. } { [%cal Gf1c4,Gd1h5] } 1... e5 { Black defends symmetrically. } 2. Qh5 { And this is the critical move- white is attacking the pawn on e5 with the queen, but notice black's f7 pawn guarded only by the king. } { [%cal Rh5e5,Rh5f7] } 2... Nc6 { This is probably black's best move. d6 and Nf6 are also good moves. This move develops the knight, while guarding the pawn on e5. } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bc6e5] } (2... g6 { This move may look OK at first glance, but this is the move that loses a rook. } 3. Qxe5+ { And there goes the rook. Black can do nothing now to defend it. } { [%cal Re5e8,Re5h8] } 3... Qe7 4. Qxh8) (2... d6 { This move may look very passive at first, since it blocks in black's bishop on f8. However, black can fianchetto it within the next few moves. } 3. Bc4 { White is now threatening checkmate. Black can easily block the attack with g6. } { [%cal Rc4f7,Rh5f7] } 3... g6 4. Qf3 { Trying for mate again! Black again can defend very easily. } { [%cal Rf3f7,Rc4f7] } 4... Nf6 { And here, black has a better position. Both his bishops will become well placed shortly, and while white's bishop on c4 is good, the queen at f3 is placed terribly, since it's developed prematurely and blocks the knight from its best square. } { [%cal Gc8g4,Gf8g7] }) (2... Nf6 { This move may look bad. White will take the pawn at e5. Why would you just give up the pawn? Well, there's a simple reason- black will gain a major advantage in development. } 3. Qxe5+ Be7 { Here black is all ready to castle, and white's queen on e5 is placed awkwardly and is going to have to move again soon. While this line isn't the BEST of the possible moves for black, it leads to an overall equal game. }) 3. Bc4 { Here white is going for checkmate! An often blunder by black is Nf6, which attacks the queen, but however lets white give checkmate. The proper move here is g6. } { [%cal Rc4f7,Rh5f7] } 3... g6 { This move kicks the queen, and guards checkmate. White can try again now with Qf3. } (3... Nf6 4. Qxf7# { Checkmate in four moves... RIP }) 4. Qf3 { And now black can defend easily by playing Nf6. } { [%cal Rf3f7,Rc4f7] } 4... Nf6 { Here black has an advantageous position. The bishop on f8 can be fianchettoed, and white's queen is placed poorly, since it blocks the knight from its best square. } 5. Qb3 { White is trying again to attack the weak f7 pawn. While it may look like black's only defense would be Qe7, black can actually win the bishop here. } { [%cal Yb3f7,Rc4f7] } 5... Nd4 { [%csl Rb3][%cal Rd4b3] } 6. Bxf7+ { White takes the pawn, assuming he can deal with the queen under attack next move. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rf7e8] } 6... Ke7 { Now, the queen has to continue to protect the bishop on f7. There's only one retreat. Also notice black is threatening a fork on c2. } { [%csl Rb3,Rd4][%cal Rd4b3,Re7f7,Rd4c2] } 7. Qc4 { This move looks like it solves all of white's problems. But black can kick the queen one more time now and win the bishop. } { [%cal Bc4c2,Bc4f7] } 7... b5 { Now white has only one retreat to protect c2 and none to guard the bishop. } { [%cal Rb5c4,Re7f7] } 8. Qc3 { [%cal Bc3c2] } 8... Kxf7 { And black, despite having his king unable to castle, has better development and a material advantage. } *


