{ Another opening very common at beginner levels, the Greco defense is another weak response to e4 e5 Nf3. The move develops the queen too early, and blocks the kingside knight from its best square. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf3e5] } 2... Qf6 { This is the Greco defense. The knight is taken from its best square, and the queen is out too early, and will not remain safe at f6 for very long. } { [%cal Bf6e5] } 3. Nc3 { This move threatens Nd5, which would force the queen to defend the c7 pawn. It also develops the knight. } { [%cal Gc3d5] } 3... Nc6 { Black develops the knight, which is necessary here, since it guards the pawn. White is going to play Nd5, attacking the queen, and will force the queen to let go of defending the e5 pawn. } 4. Nd5 Qd8 { And now black has to bring his queen back home. Qd6 would also guard the c7 pawn but it would block the kingside bishop, as well as the d-pawn from advancing. } 5. d4 exd4 6. Nxd4 { And here, it's pretty clear that white has a far better position. White's bishops can move freely about, and has two central knights and a central pawn, while black just has one central knight. } *


