{ The Latvian gambit is an extremely risky opening for black. After e4 e5 Nf3, black plays f5, attacking aggressively in the center. The move is considered unsound, since it gives away a pawn, and while black may get some attacking chances, if white knows how to play against it, he can come out of the opening with a significant advantage. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 { [%cal Rf3e5] } 2... f5 { This is the signature move of the Latvian. While many players may think taking the pawn on f5 is the best move, taking the pawn on e5 with the knight is better. } 3. Nxe5 Nf6 { Black kinda has to play this. He can't take the pawn on e4, since then white can exploit the kingside with Qh5+ } (3... fxe4 4. Qh5+ g6 5. Nxg6 hxg6 6. Qxh8 { And black now has a completely lost position. }) 4. Bc4 { This move is aiming after the f7 square to try and fork the queen and the rook. Black only two ways to avoid losing it, d5 or Qe7. Qe7 is far better though. } { [%cal Re5f7,Rc4f7] } 4... Qe7 (4... d5 5. exd5 { White now has a decisive advantage. }) 5. d4 { White defends the knight with an outpost, while gaining central space. } 5... Nc6 { This is black's best move here. Black attempts to trade off knights and then to regain the pawn on e4. } 6. O-O { This move will give back the pawn to black. But, white has castled and now has an excellent position. } 6... Nxe5 7. dxe5 Qxe5 8. exf5 Qxf5 { And here white has a clear advantage. Black has developed almost nothing, while white has safely castled, and the bishop on c4 will prevent black from castling kingside for a while. It's not completely lost for black or anything, but white has a far better position out of the opening. } *


[Event "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️: Borg Defense"]
