[ChapterName "Borg Defense"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/e01yoGqe/u4iMND1h"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Water-Flame"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.19"]
[UTCTime "15:31:36"]

{ Another response to e4, the Borg Defense basically self-destructs his kingside on move 1. Black will lose space in the center and time developing, all while his kingside will be extremely vulnerable to white attacks. }
1. e4 g5 2. d4 { White plays d4, which gets yet another central pawn, and is attacking the pawn on g5. Black has two ways to defend the pawn, or can attempt a trap, which as white, you'll need to watch out for. } { [%cal Rc1g5] } 2... e6 { This is black's best way to defend the pawn, since it opens up for the queen and the bishop is black chooses not to place it on g7. However black's position is still very weak. } { [%cal Bd8g5] } (2... f6 { This move may look like it defends the pawn, but note that there's a clear diagonal to the king... } 3. Qh5# { So, uh, well, f6 doesn't exactly work. }) (2... h6 { This move also defends the pawn and looks OK for black since it makes a little pawn chain, but white can attack this weak move with 3. h4! } 3. h4 gxh4 { Black's only viable option here is to take the pawn, and now the pawn that started on g5 looks even worse than where it was after move 1. White has no need to recapture immediately. } 4. Nf3 { Taking with the rook isn't a great move since it brings the rook out in the opening; rooks are best in the middlegame and endgame. So, white can attack the pawn with the knight. } 4... Bg7 { Black has no way to save the pawn, so instead he can just go on with developing his army. } 5. Nc3 { And now white has both knights developed, two central pawns, and open diagonals for both bishops. Black just has a bishop placed on b7, which is OK, but the rest of his position is terrible. Hanging pawns on the h-file and almost no pieces developed. Not to mention his horribly weak kingside. White has a far better position here. }) (2... Bg7 { This is black's attempt to trap white. He sacrifices his pawn on b5, hoping white will fall for a trap that will lose him a rook. } 3. Bxg5 { This move is needed for the trap to work, but it's perfectly fine. White just has to be aware of this next pawn sacrifice by black. } 3... c5 { Black is hoping white will take this pawn. White should instead defend with Nf3. } 4. Nf3 (4. dxc5 { If white takes the pawn here, black can take the pawn on b2 with his bishop, and then go on to take white's rook. } 4... Bxb2 5. Nd2 Bxa1 6. Qxa1) 4... Nc6 { Black still tries to get white to take the pawn. White can still defend it, though. } 5. Be3 { White can bring back his bishop to e3, while defending the pawn. Pushing the pawn to d5 is also acceptable. } (5. d5 Bxb2 6. Nbd2 Bxa1 7. Qxa1 { [%cal Ra1h8] } 7... Nd4 8. Nxd4 cxd4 9. Qxd4 { And white has a good position here, too. })) 3. Nc3 { White can go on with developing his knight, taking firm control of the center. } 3... Bg7 { Black places the bishop on b7, trying to attack the center from a distance. } 4. Be3 { This move locks up the center for white. Black can't really do much here. His kingside is weakened, and white is dominating the center, and black doesn't really have any good counterattacking options here. } *


[Event "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️: Dutch Defense"]
