{ The Dutch Defense is not necessarily a bad opening, but if black doesn't really know what he's doing it will lead to a quick victory for white. In most variations of the Dutch though, white will still gain a solid advantage. The Dutch weakens black's kingside quite a bit, and black needs to make sure to avoid a few traps. The Dutch is still played occasionally by Masters, though. }
1. d4 f5 { This is the critical move of the Dutch defense. A critical move in the Dutch is e6, and white can prevent black from playing it easily. } 2. Bg5 { This is the Hopton attack, which is the move I'd recommend against the Dutch. It pretty much prevents black's plans of playing e6. In the other variations of the Dutch, such as where white plays c4 or Nf3, black gets a pretty good game. This move makes it tough for black though. There is also a trap here that players new to the Dutch will fall for. } { [%cal Rg5d8] } 2... Nf6 { This is black's best move to attempt to play e6, but white can trade off the bishop for black's knight and force the doubled pawns. } (2... h6 { This move will lead to the trap. } 3. Bh4 g5 { Black tries to get the bishop away to allow e6, even though it weakens his kingside. } 4. Bg3 f4 { And it looks like white's bishop is trapped! But, there's an easy way to avoid this for white now. } 5. e3 { White is threatening checkmate! Black can't take the bishop. } { [%cal Gd1h5,Rh5e8] } 5... Nf6 { This move defends from checkmate, but now white will have a big advantage. } (5... fxg3 6. Qh5# { And... checkmate. }) 6. exf4 gxf4 7. Bxf4) 3. Bxf6 exf6 4. c4 { And here white has an excellent position. White has two central pawns, and soon can play e3, while black has two pawns on the f-file and a weakened kingside. } *


