{ The Fred Defense is another opening for black that weakens the kingside. It is similar to the Latvian in the sense that black offers white his f-pawn to try and get better development. However, with correct play, white will have an advantage. }
1. e4 f5 { This is the Fred Defense, also known as the Duras Gambit. White is best off accepting the pawn. } 2. exf5 Nf6 { Black is almost forced to play this, since he has to defend Qh5+. } { [%cal Bf6h5] } 3. Be2 { Now white is really threatening an attack on the king. Black really doesn't have many ways to avoid losing the right to castle, but there is one way. } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Ge2h5,Bd1e2] } 3... h5 { This is pretty much the only way black can prevent his king from moving. But now his kingside defense is pretty mucn nonexistent and white is ahead in development, too. } 4. Nf3 d5 { It may look like black can easily regain his pawn and develop his bishop nicely, but that is not the case. } 5. Nh4 { There's not much black can do to try and regain the pawn. Instead, he can try and exchange pawns and develop his queenside knight. } { [%cal Bh4f5] } 5... e6 6. Ng6 Rh7 7. fxe6 Bxe6 8. d4 { Black's best option here is Nc6, to try and develop his minor pieces. } 8... Nc6 9. O-O { And now white is castled, with a far superior position. Black's position is pretty terrible, while white has his king castled safely and can develop his other minor pieces shortly. White is also winning by a pawn. } *


[Event "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️: Scandinavian Defense (Qe5+ Variation)"]
