[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/e01yoGqe/4mWjcX5F"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Water-Flame"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.19"]
[UTCTime "19:56:48"]

{ The Scandinavian defense is an attempt by black to immediately respond to white's e4 move by attacking the pawn supported by the queen with d5. While the opening isn't played often by GMs due to the fact that it brings out the queen too early, it is still played occasionally. However, the Qe5+ variation is never played by GMs, but played often by beginners hoping for white to make a mistake. }
1. e4 d5 { This is the Scandinavian defense. } { [%cal Rd5e4] } 2. exd5 Qxd5 { And as you can see, black's queen is developed on move 2, and white can gain some time developing by attacking it with Nc3. } 3. Nc3 { Here black has two good options: Qa5 (The Main Line) or Qd6. However, many beginners prefer to give check with Qe5+. } 3... Qe5+ { White can now gain more time developing by playing Be2. } 4. Be2 { And here most beginners will continue to try to get white to make a mistake by playing Qg5. } 4... Qg5 5. Bf3 { This prevents Qxg2, and now black's queen is very vulnerable to attack. } 5... Qe5+ { Many beginners will try again at checking the king. And now, white can develop his knight. } 6. Nge2 Nf6 7. d4 { And now white has his pieces developed so much better than black. All black has developed is a knight and the queen, whereas white has a central pawns, a bishop, two knights and the ability to safely castle. It's pretty obvious who's winning now. } *


[Event "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️: Ruy Lopez: Nurenberg Defense"]
[Date "2017.09.19"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C60"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Nürnberg Variation"]
[StudyName "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️"]
[ChapterName "Ruy Lopez: Nurenberg Defense"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/e01yoGqe/Z4MYcQwA"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Water-Flame"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.19"]
[UTCTime "21:49:25"]

{ The Nurenberg defense against the Ruy Lopez (Spanish) opening is yet another opening where black advances his f-pawn early in the game. It's an extremely weak way to defend against the Spanish, and is therefore almost never seen in master play, but occasionally in games between amateurs. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 f6 { This is the main move of the Nurenberg defense. It's bad because it weakens the kingside and blocks the knight from it's best square. While there is no direct refutation of this opening such as in the Damiano defense, white can get a far better game just by developing normally. } 4. O-O a6 5. Bc4 { While this move may look strange due to the fact that in the Spanish white normally plays Ba4, this move is probably better, because it blocks black from castling kingside as well as putting some pressure on the diagonal to f7. } 5... Bc5 { Many players will choose to get their bishop out now, but it's not really doing that much on c5. White can now prepare a central pawn push with c3. } 6. c3 Nge7 { This is black's most common response, to attempt to castle kingside. However, white's bishop is blocking that. } { [%cal Rc4g8] } 7. d4 exd4 8. cxd4 { And now white has pretty much taken over the center of the board. Not only that, but black's kingside is weak and white can exploit this later. White should have a definitive advantage going into the middlegame. } *


[Event "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️: Napoleon's Attack"]
[Date "2017.09.19"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "C20"]
[Opening "King's Pawn Game: Napoleon Attack"]
[StudyName "Punishing 👊 Bad 😱 Openings ✌️"]
[ChapterName "Napoleon's Attack"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/e01yoGqe/8lhmUHSY"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Water-Flame"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.19"]
[UTCTime "22:00:01"]

{ This opening is similar to Parham's Attack in the sense that white develops his queen on move two and hopes for a quick checkmate on f7. However, this opening is even easier for black to defend against. }
1. e4 e5 2. Qf3 { White is obviously aiming for a checkmate in four moves. While this opening is played very rarely among masters, it is extremely popular among beginners. Black can easily defend the attack, if he knows what he is doing. } { [%cal Rf3f7,Gf1c4,Rc4f7] } 2... Nf6 3. Bc4 { And now here black can go after the center with c6, preparing a pawn push to d5. } 3... c6 4. Nc3 { White can play Nc3, preventing black from pushing his pawn to d5 while developing the knight. } { [%cal Bc3d5] } 4... Bb4 { This move may not look great at first look. Why is black exchanging his good bishop for the queenside knight? However, there's a very good reason behind this move. } 5. a3 Bxc3 6. dxc3 d5 7. exd5 cxd5 { And now black has control over the center. Black can also gain time developing next move by attacking the queen. } 8. Bb3 Bg4 { White's queen is forced to move away. g3 is its best square. } 9. Qg3 Nc6 10. Nf3 Qd6 { Black develops his queen to a relatively safe square, while guarding the pawn on e5. Black has a slight advantage here- but white still has a perfectly playable game. } *


