[UTCTime "11:40:22"]

{ While the French defense is played by many masters and is known as one of the best responses to e4, there is one variation that beginners play A LOT. It brings out the queen on move 2, and really has no intended plan. But it's still popular among beginners. }
1. e4 e6 { This is the French defense. In most variations, black prepares a pawn push from d7 to d5. However, most beginners playing this opening have a different idea. } 2. d4 Qh4 { Black brings out the queen on move two. He can't really try for the four move checkmate here, as his bishop can't move to c5, since the pawn is guarding it. White can easily defend the queen's only threat, which is the pawn on e4. } { [%cal Rh4e4] } 3. Nc3 { Here most players will stay aggressive, and attack the knight. } { [%cal Bc3e4] } 3... Bb4 4. Bd3 Nf6 { Nf6 is a common move here for black, but it's a really bad one. The queen is placed terribly on h4 and needs to retreat back, but Nf6 blocks it. The main idea of Nf6 is to keep pressuring the pawn on e4, but again, white can easily defend this attack. } { [%cal Bd3e4,Rf6e4] } 5. Nf3 { Black continues attacking, still hoping for white to make a mistake. White can just castle though, and gain even more time in development. } 5... Qg4 { [%cal Rg4g2,Rg4e4,Rf6e4] } 6. O-O Bxc3 { Black takes the knight, to double white's pawn and remove the knight defending the e4 pawn. } 7. bxc3 Nxe4 { Most players here take with the knight, even though this move is a blunder and gives white the knight for a pawn. } 8. h3 { Black's queen only has two retreats that still guard the knight: Qf5 and Qg6. Qf5 is slightly better. } 8... Qf5 9. g4 { White continues to attack black's queen. While it may look like this weakens the kingside, white should be fine. } 9... Qg6 { Black still tries to hang on to his knight. Now white can stop him, finally. } { [%cal Bg6e4] } 10. Ne5 { The queen has nowhere to go other than f6. White can now capture black's knight with an obvious advantage. } 10... Qf6 11. Bxe4 *


